        intro: "This is a story about two friends who find an unexpected visitor on the beach.",
        body: `'Over there – by the rocks!' Josh called, pointing along the beach.\n\nRuby shielded her eyes against the low morning sun. At first she saw nothing but the grey rocks at the far end of Penhallow Cove, the little Cornish bay where she and Josh had spent every summer they could remember. Then something moved.\n\nLying on the wet sand, just above the line of seaweed left by the tide, was a smooth, silvery shape. It lifted its head and looked straight at them with enormous, dark eyes, as round and shiny as marbles.\n\n'It's a seal,' Ruby breathed. 'A baby one.'\n\nJosh was already running, his flip-flops slapping against the sand. Ruby hurried after him, but something made her slow down before they reached the rocks. She grabbed the back of Josh's T-shirt.\n\n'Stop! Don't get too close.'\n\nJosh skidded to a halt, spraying sand over his feet. 'Why not? It looks friendly.'\n\nThe pup opened its mouth and let out a long, wailing cry, like a small child who had lost its mother. Its white whiskers trembled. Ruby noticed a thin, red scratch across one of its flippers, and her stomach tightened.\n\n'It's hurt,' she said quietly. 'Look at its flipper.'\n\nJosh crouched down in the sand, keeping his distance this time. 'Maybe its mum is coming back for it. Seals leave their babies on the beach sometimes, don't they? I saw it on a nature programme.'\n\nRuby chewed her lip. Josh was right – she had heard that too. But the tide was already on its way out, and the pup was lying further up the beach than the water would reach for hours. Above them, a pair of herring gulls circled lower and lower, screaming to each other.\n\n'What if its mum doesn't come back?' she said. 'Those gulls look hungry.'\n\nJosh glanced up at the sky and frowned. 'We could carry it back into the sea,' he suggested, reaching out a hand.\n\n'No!' Ruby said, more sharply than she had meant to. Josh snatched his hand back as if he had touched a hot pan. 'Sorry,' she added. 'It's just – Gran always says you should never touch a seal. They can bite really hard, and if people handle the pups, their mums might not want them any more.'\n\nRuby's gran had lived in Penhallow all her life and volunteered with the marine rescue team in the village. Ruby had once seen her kneel beside a stranded dolphin for three hours, pouring buckets of sea water over its skin until the rescue boat arrived.\n\nRuby pulled her phone out of her pocket. One bar of signal. She held it high above her head, turning slowly in a circle, until a second bar flickered into view.\n\n'Gran? It's Ruby. We've found a seal pup on the beach, and it's hurt.'\n\nShe listened, nodding, while Josh watched the pup. It had stopped crying and was watching them both with a puzzled expression, its head tilted to one side.\n\n'She's coming,' Ruby said at last, pushing the phone back into her pocket. 'She says we've done exactly the right thing. We've got to stay back, keep the gulls away and not let anyone walk their dog near it.'\n\nJosh grinned and pulled off his baseball cap. He waved it wildly at the gulls, which wheeled away across the bay. 'Guard duty,' he announced, sitting down cross-legged on the sand. 'Nobody gets past us.'\n\nRuby sat down beside him. The pup gave a small, snuffling sigh and rested its chin on the sand, and for the first time that morning, Ruby let herself smile.`
