        title: "Nia Reaches for the Stars",
        intro: "This is an extract from a book about a girl called Nia, who loves space and dreams of becoming an astronaut.",
        body: `My name is Nia Okafor and I am eleven and three-quarters. You're supposed to describe yourself at the start of a book, so here goes.\n\nI am tall for my age (useful for reaching the top shelf in the library, where they keep all the best space books). I have black hair in two puffs, which Mum says makes me look like I'm wearing a pair of headphones. I always wear my silver trainers, because they are the closest thing I own to a spacesuit. In my rucksack there is always a torch with a red filter, a star map and a notebook with a rocket on the front.\n\nI AM READY FOR LIFT-OFF AT ANY MOMENT.\n\nMy heroes are:\n\nTIM PEAKE. He was the first British astronaut to visit the International Space Station, and he ran a whole marathon on a treadmill while he was up there. In space! I can't even run to the bus stop without getting a stitch.\n\nCAROLINE HERSCHEL. More than two hundred years ago, when most people didn't think girls should be scientists at all, she discovered comets by staying up night after night with a telescope. (Nobody has made a film about her. Someone should. I'd watch it twice.)\n\nMy Auntie Grace is the reason I love space. She lives on a farm in the middle of nowhere, which sounds boring but is actually brilliant, because there are no street lights. On a clear night, the sky above the farm isn't black at all – it's crowded with so many stars that it looks as if someone has spilled a bag of sugar across a dark tablecloth.\n\nEvery summer, Auntie Grace and I drag two old deckchairs into the middle of the cow field, wrap ourselves in blankets and wait. She taught me that you have to give your eyes twenty minutes to get used to the dark, and that you should never, ever look at your phone, because one glance at the bright screen and you have to start all over again. That's what the red torch is for – red light doesn't ruin your night vision. (Auntie Grace knows everything.)\n\nThe best thing about space? It never ends. Literally. However much you learn, there is always more out there, waiting to be discovered. Auntie Grace says that every scientist starts with a question, and that the most important words in science are 'I wonder'.\n\nWhich brings me to the most exciting news of my entire life so far.\n\nLast Monday, Mr Adebayo pinned a notice to our classroom wall:\n\n> CALLING ALL YOUNG SCIENTISTS!\nCan you explain a mystery of the universe?\nDesign a project and present it at our Science Fair.\nThe winner will visit the National Space Centre!\nSee Mr Adebayo for details.\n\nI read it three times. Then I read it again, just to be sure. The National Space Centre! There was only one problem: I had about a million questions about the universe, and I had to choose just one.`
