        id: 3, label: "Text 3 — Non-fiction",
        title: "Mary Anning: The Fossil Hunter",
        intro: "This text is about a woman whose discoveries changed what people knew about the history of life on Earth.",
        body: `## Fossils and the Jurassic Coast\n\nAlong the south coast of England, stretching from east Devon into Dorset, lies a line of crumbling cliffs known today as the Jurassic Coast. Around the town of Lyme Regis, the rocks are roughly 200 million years old, and they are packed with fossils – the preserved remains of animals and plants that lived long ago. Each winter, storms and heavy rain wash away parts of the cliffs, revealing fossils that have been hidden for millions of years.\n\nTwo hundred years ago, very few people understood what fossils really were. Some believed they were simply strangely shaped stones; others thought they belonged to creatures still living in unexplored parts of the world. The idea that whole species could have died out – become extinct – was new and hotly debated. One young woman from Lyme Regis would help to change people's minds.\n\n## A childhood on the cliffs\n\nMary Anning was born in Lyme Regis in 1799. Her family was poor, and her father, Richard, was a carpenter who earned extra money by collecting fossils from the beach and selling them to tourists as 'curiosities'. He taught Mary and her brother Joseph how to search the cliffs and how to clean the fossils they found.\n\nIt was dangerous work. Collectors had to hunt for fossils after storms, when the cliffs were most likely to collapse, and they had to keep a careful eye on the tide. When Mary was eleven, her father died, leaving the family in debt. From then on, the money that Mary and Joseph earned from fossils helped to keep the family going.\n\n## The first great discovery\n\nIn around 1811, Joseph found a huge skull sticking out of the cliff. Over the following months, Mary carefully uncovered the rest of the skeleton. It was over five metres long and looked a little like a crocodile with the body of a fish. It was later named an ichthyosaur, meaning 'fish lizard'. Scientists had never seen anything quite like it, and it caused great excitement.\n\nMary went on to make many more remarkable finds. In 1823, she uncovered the first complete skeleton of a plesiosaur, a sea reptile with a tiny head, a long neck and four large flippers. Some experts were so astonished by its strange shape that they suspected it was a fake – until they examined it for themselves. In 1828, she found the first pterosaur – a flying reptile – ever discovered in Britain.\n\n## A scientist without credit\n\nMary did not simply find fossils; she studied them. She taught herself geology and anatomy, and she even dissected modern fish and cuttlefish so that she could compare them with the ancient creatures she found. Visiting scientists were often surprised by how much she knew.\n\nHowever, as a woman from a poor family, Mary was not allowed to join the Geological Society of London, where scientists met to discuss new ideas. Wealthy collectors bought her fossils and displayed them in museums, and scientists wrote about them, but Mary's name was rarely mentioned. 'The world has used me so unkindly,' she once wrote in a letter.\n\n## Mary's legacy\n\nMary Anning died in 1847, aged 47. In the years that followed, her work helped scientists to understand that the Earth was extremely old and that many creatures that once lived on it had become extinct. Today, she is recognised as one of the most important fossil hunters in history. Her fossils can be seen at the Natural History Museum in London, and in 2022, a statue of Mary, with her hammer and her dog, Tray, was unveiled in Lyme Regis – 175 years after her death.`
